title: Servant of All – Serving and Loving Others
description: The Christian walk, part four: charity the more excellent way, pure religion toward the fatherless, the widow and the poor, forgiving as we are forgiven, loving our enemies, the greatest as servant of all, and a good witness before men, from the teachings on serving and loving others.
output: lessons/doctrine/topic-walk-serving.html
highlight: serve

# The fourth of five decks on the walk (the user's call): "Serving and loving others" and "A good witness before men".
# Slide text is the author's own sentences, trimmed. The verses the teachings typed out are shown from the KJV.
# Fixed: "Who are week" -> "weak" (9477), "fake and gay" line and other asides left out (953, 4207, 5873, 10898, 17156).

=== Introduction
--- Servant of All [title]
overline: Doctrine · The walk
heading: Servant of All
sub: The walk, part four: serving and loving others, with a good witness before men.
refs: Mark 10:44-45; James 1:27; Romans 12:21; Matthew 5:16
hint: Use the arrow keys or swipe to move. Tap any gold reference to read the verse.

# from 1190, 1188
--- The cross I take up daily
big: Romans 15:1 | mark: We then that are strong ought to bear the infirmities of the weak
p: The turning from sin and giving up worldly pleasures and habits is actually the easiest part. Living for God, and loving others as ourselves, magnifying the patience and love He has shown us, that's the challenge. That's the cross I take up daily.
p: With those who become a burden, it becomes so difficult. But in it there's a lesson for those of us willing to see, how much worse it must be for God dealing with us.
read: app:doctrine-walk~1190 | The cross I take up daily
read: app:doctrine-walk~1188 | Patience with the weak as God with us

=== Charity, the more excellent way
# from 3905, 3906
--- The more excellent way
kicker: Charity
big: 1 Corinthians 12:31 | mark: and yet shew I unto you a more excellent way
lead: It is the unfeigned love, given for no personal gain, but genuine and selfless, that is above all else, and is a faint reflection of the love God showed us by giving His Son that we may be saved.
p: The charity is caring for the others in the church, sorrow for each members' sorrow, and joy for their individual success. Not coveting the gifts each may have. Edifying one another.

# from 3905, 17874
--- The motive, not the gift
kicker: Charity
big: 1 Corinthians 13:3 | mark: and have not charity, it profiteth me nothing
p: We usually use the basic definition of charity to mean giving something away to help others, alms. That can be a form of expression for charity, but it is not itself charity. So many do so much as a form of “charity” but for selfish purpose, or taking pride or accolades for it.
p: Helping people who cannot help you back out of love, is love unfeigned. The spirit behind it is everything. We can know all things in scripture, and if we aren't edifying others and given to love people, it's for nothing. It's the motive not the gift.

# from 3908
--- Faith, hope and charity abide
kicker: Charity
big: 1 Corinthians 13:13 | mark: the greatest of these is charity
p: Charity, the unconditional love that is God's love, magnified through us, that cares for others and seeks to build them up, even at our own expense. These are what endure in the Kingdom of God.
p: Not tongues, various languages or soaring rhetoric, not preaching and prophecy, that fails and fades. Not knowledge, that is imperfect at best in our current state, seeing through the glass darkly.

# from 10898
--- Love without dissimulation
kicker: Charity
big: Romans 12:9 | mark: Abhor that which is evil; cleave to that which is good
p: Dissimulation is deceit, dishonesty regarding intent. Concealing motives. Speaking the truth in love. There is no love in manipulation or in hidden agendas.
p: What men call love is often manipulative and controlling, and with this false love comes the lack of judgment, calling nothing evil. That isn't love. God's love is without manipulation, freely given. And God hates evil. It is impossible to love without hate.
read: app:doctrine-walk~3905 | Charity, the more excellent way
read: app:doctrine-walk~10898 | Let love be without dissimulation

=== Pure religion
# from 9228, 12848, 16996, 17700
--- A mission statement
kicker: Pure religion
big: Luke 4:18 | mark: to heal the brokenhearted
p: This reading of Isaiah by Christ Jesus, a prophecy of Him fulfilled before those in the synagogue that day, serves as a mission statement for Christians and our purpose as servants of God.
p: Jesus moved with compassion for the widow of Nain. A woman he doesn't know. No one has sought him to intervene. He does what He can to comfort the brokenhearted ([[Luke 7:12-15]]).

# from 12848, 16996, 17700
--- To visit the fatherless and widows
kicker: Pure religion
big: James 1:27 | mark: To visit the fatherless and widows in their affliction
p: Religion is the outward practice of the faith we have inwardly. We are meant to serve. To help. To feed and clothe the needy, both physically and spiritually. Not just pray “be ye warmed and filled,” but to genuinely care about others.
p: A lot of people seem to think that Jesus introduced these concepts in the New Testament. But again and again we see that this is how God wants us to be. Self-sacrifice for others, not the selfish sacrifice of the works-based religions of the world ([[Hosea 6:6]]; [[Proverbs 21:3]]).

# from 9469
--- Workers of Heaven
kicker: Pure religion
big: Matthew 23:23 | mark: omitted the weightier matters of the law, judgment, mercy, and faith
lead: We aren't supposed to be working *for* Heaven, we are supposed to be workers *of* Heaven.
p: That the Kingdom of Heaven draw nigh them, when we do the charity and humble service to others we are called to. When we give the Gospel to the poor, and heal the broken hearted. Clothe the naked and feed those in need.
p: We didn't find Jesus, He found us, and set us to labor for Him, as we were bought with a price.

# from 9477, 17874
--- Call the poor to the feast
kicker: Pure religion
big: Luke 14:13-14 | mark: call the poor, the maimed, the lame, the blind
p: If you're invited to a party, don't go in assuming you're asked to sit in the high places of status, instead be seated in the lowest room ([[Luke 14:10]]).
p: If you have the means to call a feast, humbly seek to serve those who cannot do anything for you. Who are weak. Honor them without thought for reward in this life and be rewarded for it at the judgment.

# from 11393, 17375, 17433
--- Leave the gleanings
kicker: Pure religion
big: Leviticus 19:10 | mark: thou shalt leave them for the poor and stranger
p: The biblical way of caring for the poor and the sojourners among the people. Not going back again and again to make sure you got all the crops that God had blessed us with. Our bounty, freely given as God so freely gave to us. In charity and mercy. Hospitality.
p: Right judgment doesn't mean weak judgment. Provide the opportunity for gleaning our fields. Be generous and hospitable, while maintaining the biblical truth; *that if any would not work, neither should he eat* ([[2 Thessalonians 3:10]]).

# from 4207, 17433
--- Personal and local
kicker: Pure religion
lead: A Godly nation takes care of the poor and widows indeed, providing sound judgement and aid to the fatherless, through the actions of individual Christians, organized locally by their church.
p: When that lacks, then inequities abound, and the beast system will use the opportunity to provide as a means of control and punishment.
p: Feeding and clothing the needy is an opportunity to do unto them and by doing so, doing it unto God. Therefore, it should be personal and local. Acts of charity and alms done discreetly, or by our churches. Not given off to the government to do for us. Biblical Christianity, not “social justice.”

# from 17240, 17203, 17874
--- Lending unto the Lord
kicker: Pure religion
big: Proverbs 19:17 | mark: He that hath pity upon the poor lendeth unto the LORD
xr: Matthew 25:40 | Inasmuch as ye have done it unto one of the least of these my brethren, ye have done it unto me.
xr: James 2:1-5 | It is human nature to be respecters of persons. To favor the wealthy for what they could do for you, while the poor is separated from their neighbors.
xr: Psalms 41:1 | Blessed is he that considereth the poor: the LORD will deliver him in time of trouble.
p: Not blindly enabling sloth and rewarding scoffers and fools, but having mercy on the poor. We are lending to the Lord. He will repay.

# from 16859, 16866, 16901, 16925
--- Comfort the broken hearted
kicker: Pure religion
big: Job 13:5 | mark: O that ye would altogether hold your peace
p: “I know you're going through a lot, so I don't want to offend you, but I can't help myself...” What follows it rarely is a help. Eliphaz was quoting scripture, and some of what he said is true. But he's misapplying it ([[Job 4:7]]).
p: When dealing with someone in grief and inconsolably miserable, it is better to sit in silence and love them, than seek to explain away their feelings, and lecture them what to do.
p: When bad things happen to friends, it is an opportunity given by God to show His love. Not make it about ourselves.

# from 16989
--- A father to the poor
kicker: Pure religion
big: Job 29:15-16 | mark: I was a father to the poor
p: Why did Job have everyone's respect? He delivered the poor and the fatherless. Those that had no man. He blessed the dying and gave to the widow in her need. Because he clothed himself in righteousness and wore judgment like a crown ([[Job 29:12-14]]).
p: Job not only was as a father to the poor, but further seeking out causes to take up that he hadn't known yet. Going out of his way to help others.
read: app:doctrine-walk~12848 | Pure religion: visit the fatherless and widows
read: app:doctrine-walk~9469 | Workers of Heaven, not working for Heaven
read: app:doctrine-walk~16925 | Comfort the broken hearted, don't berate them

=== Forgive as forgiven
# from 5335
--- Seventy times seven
kicker: Forgive
big: Matthew 18:21-22 | mark: Until seventy times seven
p: Jesus replies, 70 times 7, indicating an order of magnitude greater than the letter of the law. To essentially always forgive those seeking it.
p: The servant beseeching the king to be forgiven a massive debt then refuses to release a fellow servant a relatively minor one ([[Matthew 18:32-33]]). Holding a grudge against one who has sincerely asked to be forgiven is evidence of pride, and a sign one has no understanding of the humility of receiving grace from God.
take: We forgive as we have been forgiven. There but for the grace of God go I.

# from 5337, 14362
--- If God forgives so much
kicker: Forgive
big: Matthew 6:14-15 | mark: if ye forgive not men their trespasses, neither will your Father forgive your trespasses
p: Jesus says that all manner of sin and blasphemy, including blasphemies against him and God the Father, shall be forgiven of men ([[Matthew 12:31]]). If our creator and savior can forgive great offense directed at them, how can we not do likewise for the petty squabbles of this life?
p: It can be hard to not lose sight of the mercy of God that has been given to us. To forget the pattern of prayer Jesus taught us, asking the Father to *forgive us our debts, as we forgive our debtors*.

# from 6059
--- Put that on mine account
kicker: Forgive
big: Philemon 1:16-18 | mark: put that on mine account
p: Paul's delicate appeal to take back the servant, not only as a servant, but forgiven as a brother in Christ is a model for us to follow. Paul even offers to pay whatever debt is owed, though he gets in a not-so-subtle reminder that Philemon owes even more so ([[Philemon 1:19]]).
p: We should also make overtures to bring brethren together, to help broker peace and forgiveness. Being peacemakers.

# from 9506
--- Correct in love, rejoice over the lost
kicker: Forgive
big: Luke 15:32 | mark: this thy brother was dead, and is alive again
p: This is not to say we ignore sin, or compromise what is right. There is church discipline for a reason. Right is right.
p: But we correct in love. We deal with the beam in our eye so that we may help others with the mote that is in theirs. We forgive when sincere apology is made. We forget, so that there is no resentment or grudge. We celebrate the return of the lost, rejoicing in their salvation.

# from 17241, 14153, 17061
--- A glory to pass over a transgression
kicker: Forgive
big: Proverbs 19:11 | mark: it is his glory to pass over a transgression
p: Let the small stuff go, let go of a slight. Our pride and anger at being treated poorly or stolen from can be difficult to resist. But it is a glory to pass over such things.
p: David's kind remembrance of Saul, someone who had tried to kill him multiple times, shows the forgiveness Christians should have ([[2 Samuel 1:23-25]]). In the death of those who've hurt us, we should let go of the animosity, because we know that God is just and He will repay.
p: It was Job's forgiveness of others who had wronged him that led God to restore him ([[Job 42:10]]).
read: app:doctrine-walk~5335 | Seventy times seven: forgive as forgiven
read: app:doctrine-walk~6059 | Philemon and Onesimus: brokering forgiveness
read: app:doctrine-walk~17061 | Job restored when he prayed for his friends

=== Love your enemies
# from 9209
--- Love your enemies, in context
kicker: Love your enemies
big: Luke 6:35-36 | mark: love ye your enemies
p: This is the part of the preaching of Christ that those who don't understand call “weak.” They remove the context. But He's just condemned the very masses He is serving, for their fickleness and worldly lusts ([[Luke 6:24-26]]).
p: Pity your enemies. They don't have what you got. We were that unthankful and evil. Sure we love those who love us. But sinners do all that too. That's easy.
p: He isn't saying to simply stand for evil, not judging the actions of others, but to put in perspective their condition with the understanding of our own. Vengeance is His.

# from 10911, 15482
--- Coals of fire on his head
kicker: Love your enemies
big: Romans 12:20-21 | mark: thou shalt heap coals of fire on his head; overcome evil with good
p: Not in the sense our detractors claim; the weakness that they perceive from this. But in truth, its strength. 9 times out of 10 when someone offends you and you turn the other cheek, which isn't an act of submission but of defiance, most people back down. Making an enemy into a friend.
p: Note the spiritual application. Your enemy hungers and thirsts. For what? Food and water? Or truth? Will you withhold that bread that came down from Heaven? Don't be as Jonah ([[Jonah 4:11]]).

# from 14083, 14084, 14187
--- David spares Saul
kicker: Love your enemies
big: 1 Samuel 24:12 | mark: the LORD avenge me of thee: but mine hand shall not be upon thee
p: When David has the opportunity to end this one-sided feud with King Saul, he chooses to let God judge. By trusting in the Lord, without a single blow, David has been delivered and his enemy made weak.
p: David's men saw the opportunity to exact vengeance as a sign from God ([[1 Samuel 24:4]]). It can be hard to do the right thing in the presence of that kind of peer pressure. Do what's right in His eyes. The rest takes care of itself.

# from 14169, 13109, 10910, 17156
--- Blessed are the peacemakers
kicker: Love your enemies
big: Romans 12:18 | mark: as much as lieth in you, live peaceably with all men
xr: Matthew 5:9 | There is an honor in being a peacemaker, regardless of the bitterness of the disagreement. Especially between brothers.
xr: Deuteronomy 20:10 | Lead with an offer of peace. It isn't a show of weakness. It is a show of strength. It is a mercy.
xr: Proverbs 3:30 | Love our enemies, certainly, but don't go out of your way to make one.
p: You don't seek vengeance because you know that belongs to God, and He is not slack when it comes to delivering just punishment on the wicked.
read: app:doctrine-walk~9209 | Love your enemies, in context
read: app:doctrine-walk~10911 | Heap coals of fire on his head
read: app:doctrine-walk~14083 | Love your enemies and let God avenge

=== Servant of all
# from 6288, 14907
--- Whosoever will be great
kicker: Servant of all
big: Mark 10:43-45 | mark: whosoever of you will be the chiefest, shall be servant of all
p: It is not about being lifted up in greatness like the rulers of this world, who are served by many, but being a lowly servant to many. Just as He, the Son of God was for us, a sacrifice.
p: And Jesus promises us, as good and trusty servants, that in His return He will again serve us ([[Luke 12:37]]). If He, the King of Kings, can humble Himself to serve us, how can we do anything less to those we meet every day?

# from 14342, 14907
--- The king as servant
kicker: Servant of all
big: Philippians 2:3-4 | mark: let each esteem other better than themselves
p: David strove to rule as a God fearing judge, with mercy and faith, putting the profit of others before himself. David is a picture of the king as servant. A picture of Christ, and an example for us who follow after Jesus.
p: We can do great things for the Lord, even strive with one another to out do each other. But first we should seek to serve. A good judge for the children, the fatherless, the widows. A servant to our brethren.

# from 9595, 9596, 9597
--- The one or the nine
kicker: Servant of all
big: Luke 17:10 | mark: We are unprofitable servants: we have done that which was our duty to do
p: All ten lepers were healed. All who believe are given eternal life. But it is the rare few who follow it up with this level of devotion as pictured by the Samaritan ([[Luke 17:15-17]]).
p: Turn from sin, read your Bible, say your prayers, love God, love others. Bare minimum Christianity. We grow our faith not by doing the bare minimum but realizing that doing it makes us unprofitable servants.
p: Are we like the 9? Saved, for certain, but doing what we ought to by obligation, and little else. This hits hard. I fall short. But it's the lesson that is being taught.

# from 10527, 10463
--- Not what is in it for me
kicker: Servant of all
big: Exodus 38:8 | mark: of the lookingglasses of the women assembling
p: The women gave their mirrors that they be crafted into the laver. Turning from self, as these faithful women did by giving up the objects of their vanity. We are asked to turn from ourselves towards service to others.
p: We Christians often approach the Bible with a “what is in it for me” attitude. We dread work, family obligations, being hospitable and neighborly. Instead we should submit to serving God in everything, as glad servants to Him.

# from 12846, 12849
--- The gift of helps
kicker: Servant of all
big: 1 Corinthians 12:28 | mark: helps
p: Though it is the easiest of the gifts to be manifest in a believer, it is often the most neglected, the least coveted, the one responsibility so many shirk. (Of which I am chief...)
p: Show up to church, not to feed our own needs, but looking whereby we can be a blessing. Asking what needs be done, with cheer. Rejoicing in our liberty that we have the opportunity to be a help to the Kingdom of God itself ([[Romans 16:1-4]]).

# from 15024, 13080, 4204
--- All things come of thee
kicker: Servant of all
big: 1 Chronicles 29:14 | mark: all things come of thee, and of thine own have we given thee
p: All we have *cometh of thine hand, and is all thine own.* Not just 10%, pretax income. All of it. Everything we have is His because it is by His grace we have it at all.
p: We who have are expected to share with brethren who lack. Our liberality leads to our receiving abundance of the spiritual. It's an even trade, not a burden ([[2 Corinthians 8:14]]; [[Deuteronomy 16:17]]).
p: So give joyfully, knowing it's not yours but the Lord's.

# from 9083, 13138, 17156, 14063, 17224
--- Love thy neighbour
kicker: Servant of all
big: Luke 3:10-11 | mark: He that hath two coats, let him impart to him that hath none
p: In your daily life, don't defraud people. Don't steal. Don't abuse whatever authority you have, at work or at home. Don't lie. Love others the way you want to be loved.
p: Do we pull over to help a person with a flat tire? Do we find someone's wallet and return it, intact ([[Deuteronomy 22:1-4]])?
p: Be a friend to others. Be as a brother, a help in trouble. Born for adversity ([[Proverbs 17:17]]). Jonathan loves David more than his own earthly reward. We should love our brothers in Christ this much ([[1 Samuel 20:17]]).
